1
00:00:04,644 --> 00:00:10,708
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anyway, that's what you were speaking about today, yesterday.

2
00:00:11,469 --> 00:00:12,750
[SPEAKER_00]: It's Samatha.

3
00:00:15,031 --> 00:00:16,312
[SPEAKER_00]: How to practice Samatha.

4
00:00:18,934 --> 00:00:20,215
[SPEAKER_01]: How to calm down.

5
00:00:20,275 --> 00:00:25,438
[SPEAKER_00]: In regards to the breath.

6
00:00:25,939 --> 00:00:33,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, especially because we talk, as I said, we talk a lot about the right endurance and sense restraint.

7
00:00:34,225 --> 00:00:41,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And every gradual... every description of the gradual training in the suttas starts with that.

8
00:00:41,392 --> 00:00:51,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you won't be doing proper samādhi unless you're fully restrained, curbed and self-controlled.

9
00:00:51,842 --> 00:00:54,945
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that is a... like seeing the danger in the slightest fault, then

10
00:00:55,585 --> 00:00:58,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Not following things with eyes, ears, nose and so on.

11
00:00:58,706 --> 00:01:00,446
[SPEAKER_01]: And eating in moderation, exactly.

12
00:01:00,466 --> 00:01:04,607
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, that leads to the kind of watchfulness and then samādhi and so on.

13
00:01:05,228 --> 00:01:09,709
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but it might create an impression that, okay, so it's just about endurance, endurance.

14
00:01:10,389 --> 00:01:11,829
[SPEAKER_01]: A lot, it's about endurance.

15
00:01:11,949 --> 00:01:18,811
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, there is a point where you can calm, calm within the endurance, calm yourself within the endurance.

16
00:01:18,911 --> 00:01:22,532
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, the same endurance is not unpleasant.

17
00:01:23,942 --> 00:01:29,512
[SPEAKER_01]: That patient endurance, as the foremost austerity, it does not need to be felt unpleasantly.

18
00:01:30,754 --> 00:01:36,864
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the difference in the suttas when it talks about the...

19
00:01:38,402 --> 00:01:48,446
[SPEAKER_01]: Like Maha Moggallāna, his way to enlightenment was quick and pleasant, as in, it didn't take long, because he was the master of the right samatha, the right calming.

20
00:01:49,146 --> 00:02:00,530
[SPEAKER_01]: So, although he went through the same process of, you know, abandoning kilesa and everything else that would make people go mad, for him, he didn't feel any displeasure, because his mind was so unshakable.

21
00:02:00,550 --> 00:02:06,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Sariputta, on the other hand, the foremost in wisdom after the Buddha, he said his way was long and unpleasant.

22
00:02:08,054 --> 00:02:11,795
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he had to endure more pain, on account of that patient endurance.

23
00:02:11,935 --> 00:02:15,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, he still had samādhi and jhānas and so on, and when he did, he became enlightened.

24
00:02:16,497 --> 00:02:24,300
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the role of that samatha, which often goes with vipassana, is obviously significant and crucial, in a way.

25
00:02:24,940 --> 00:02:29,922
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I was always saying, you can't meditate rightly, unless you have the right view.

26
00:02:31,142 --> 00:02:33,304
[SPEAKER_01]: That statement can be a bit too abstract for people.

27
00:02:34,064 --> 00:02:46,733
[SPEAKER_01]: So, practically, the reason why you won't be meditating rightly, without a right view, is because your meditation, your samatha, will not be calming of the endurance, it will be rooted in aversion towards endurance.

28
00:02:47,474 --> 00:02:53,058
[SPEAKER_01]: It will be rooted in you trying to get rid of the discomfort of endurance through this meditation technique.

29
00:02:54,453 --> 00:02:55,834
[SPEAKER_01]: And means you don't endure.

30
00:02:56,074 --> 00:03:03,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're ignoring... well, you're blocking the direction through which samatha would work, calm you and make your way to enlightenment pleasant.

31
00:03:03,877 --> 00:03:05,758
[SPEAKER_00]: Make your endurance pleasant.

32
00:03:05,918 --> 00:03:07,879
[SPEAKER_01]: So, endurance is not negotiable.

33
00:03:08,950 --> 00:03:21,360
[SPEAKER_01]: How much you're gonna suffer on account of it, how much your mind will be tormented, yeah, that depends on individual, depending on your faculties, if you're more about, like, vipassana side, or if you're more about calming down, but it needs to be done.

34
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:24,263
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, again, gradual way always starts

35
00:03:25,747 --> 00:03:37,632
[SPEAKER_01]: By sīla, seeing the danger in the slightest fault, full sense restraint, not giving in to sensuality, acting out of ill will, distraction, moderation in eating, results in watchfulness, results in build-up of endurance.

36
00:03:37,792 --> 00:03:43,355
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, within that, you can learn what calmness is, what true samatha is.

37
00:03:44,355 --> 00:03:51,358
[SPEAKER_01]: But not at the expense or not like, oh, I'll deal with endurance, the pain of it, I'll deal with the discomfort, I'll manage it.

38
00:03:52,058 --> 00:03:55,440
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for most people, without the right view, that's what meditation is.

39
00:03:57,063 --> 00:04:02,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, it just becomes another management, another cover-up, that's rooted in aversion towards discomfort.

40
00:04:03,748 --> 00:04:09,633
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're not uprooting anything.

41
00:04:11,254 --> 00:04:17,139
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one is enduring, not giving in to any defilement.

42
00:04:19,001 --> 00:04:20,622
[SPEAKER_00]: And the pressure is there.

43
00:04:22,243 --> 00:04:24,585
[SPEAKER_00]: Pressuring, pressuring, but you're not giving in.

44
00:04:25,065 --> 00:04:25,746
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not giving in.

45
00:04:27,523 --> 00:04:27,923
[SPEAKER_01]: There you go.

46
00:04:28,444 --> 00:04:29,205
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your endurance.

47
00:04:29,625 --> 00:04:32,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, again, you have a pressure, and that's why it's pressure.

48
00:04:33,609 --> 00:04:38,473
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, but just, you know, do this or do that, in order to get rid of the pressure.

49
00:04:39,053 --> 00:04:40,735
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you see that, you feel that.

50
00:04:40,855 --> 00:04:43,798
[SPEAKER_01]: Whatever you've been doing, you've been acting in order to get rid of the pressure.

51
00:04:44,398 --> 00:04:53,827
[SPEAKER_01]: So, while you're all full of doubt, conflicted, giving in, or refusing to give in, tempted, pressured, enduring the pain, what else is there?

52
00:04:55,124 --> 00:05:11,470
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are kind of aware of, and you can be aware of, and it's enduring, and you're doing it, and it doesn't require effort, and it doesn't require special intention, and it cannot become something you do in order to get rid of this or that, because it's just too subtle of an action.

53
00:05:11,650 --> 00:05:15,871
[SPEAKER_01]: What is there happening while you're all tormented with the pressure you're not giving in?

54
00:05:18,632 --> 00:05:19,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Breathing in, breathing out.

55
00:05:20,453 --> 00:05:21,113
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what's happening.

56
00:05:23,393 --> 00:05:30,900
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's the the ānāpānasati, whenever we describe it, it's like he knows, like knowingly he breathes in, knowingly.

57
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:36,445
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's about the knowledge of your breath that it's already there, happening to the body, so to speak.

58
00:05:36,485 --> 00:05:41,550
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what we speak, that's what we say, like the peripheral.

59
00:05:42,633 --> 00:05:53,578
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like, okay, so, you might, you might even, like, you know, if you're all wound up through that endurance and senses pressuring you and so on, you might feel like, okay, so, I'll breathe in like this, I'll breathe in like... yeah, whatever, do it.

60
00:05:53,839 --> 00:05:58,761
[SPEAKER_01]: But at the same time, just know that you can only do that because the breath is already there, breathing.

61
00:06:00,251 --> 00:06:01,051
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's effortless.

62
00:06:01,171 --> 00:06:02,351
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's a neutral action.

63
00:06:02,732 --> 00:06:11,154
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an action that cannot truly, if you see it in that peripheral sense, like, if you just know it, not necessarily do it, because you don't need to do it in order to breathe.

64
00:06:11,194 --> 00:06:16,635
[SPEAKER_01]: You're already breathing while you're trying to do all these other things, including enduring, getting rid of the endurance, and so on.

65
00:06:17,395 --> 00:06:19,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, oh, all I need to do...

66
00:06:19,255 --> 00:06:22,716
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to do this in order for it to be the action that will calm me down.

67
00:06:22,836 --> 00:06:26,977
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is know it happening underneath whatever's going on here.

68
00:06:27,577 --> 00:06:28,518
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness of breathing

69
00:06:31,558 --> 00:06:32,839
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what means put it first.

70
00:06:34,079 --> 00:06:37,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Put it before whatever else you're occupied with.

71
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:40,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in response to, not at the expense of, and so on.

72
00:06:41,461 --> 00:06:44,582
[SPEAKER_01]: And even now, while we are talking, breathing is there.

73
00:06:44,882 --> 00:06:47,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And you can know, even while you're talking, that you're still breathing in, breathing out.

74
00:06:47,783 --> 00:06:48,643
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to control it.

75
00:06:49,023 --> 00:06:49,724
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to stop it.

76
00:06:50,064 --> 00:06:50,884
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need to stop.

77
00:06:51,064 --> 00:06:51,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I breathing?

78
00:06:51,624 --> 00:06:52,264
[SPEAKER_01]: I know I'm breathing.

79
00:06:52,825 --> 00:06:53,665
[SPEAKER_01]: However vaguely,

80
00:06:54,450 --> 00:06:57,373
[SPEAKER_01]: There is still the thread of the knowledge of the same breath.

81
00:06:58,053 --> 00:06:59,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Breathing in, breathing out.

82
00:06:59,574 --> 00:07:05,539
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's that thread of that peripheral knowledge that doesn't require any action, just pure recognition.

83
00:07:05,559 --> 00:07:08,342
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how you start to practice ānāpānasati in the right direction.

84
00:07:08,522 --> 00:07:11,465
[SPEAKER_01]: As a... just something you're aware of that's there.

85
00:07:11,485 --> 00:07:13,066
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not doing it in response to this.

86
00:07:13,306 --> 00:07:16,489
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there happening and you just choose to not forget it.

87
00:07:17,009 --> 00:07:17,370
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

88
00:07:18,170 --> 00:07:20,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Within this pressure or whatever else.

89
00:07:21,314 --> 00:07:32,742
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you cultivate sufficiently enough that not forgetting of the breath, recollectedness of the breath, mindfulness of the breath, that's why it's sati, it will calm down any hindrance that was there occupying you or pressuring you.

90
00:07:33,222 --> 00:07:46,412
[SPEAKER_01]: It will take away that first-person, like, engagement with this pressure, with this hindrance, and I must do this, I must do this, and it's just... things will be just seen for what they are, enduring there, neither pleasant nor unpleasant, neutral.

91
00:07:47,643 --> 00:07:52,288
[SPEAKER_01]: And that in itself would be very pleasant, obviously, because you just relieve yourself of pressure without trying to get rid of it.

92
00:07:54,930 --> 00:07:55,871
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the true samatha.

93
00:07:55,911 --> 00:07:57,413
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you're like, oh, yeah, that's samatha you can do.

94
00:07:58,154 --> 00:08:09,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you have the right view now, that prevents you from trying to manage at any expense, that recognizes within the endurance I need to find the calmness, and that will then force you in the direction of what true calmness is.

95
00:08:11,670 --> 00:08:13,571
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you know, there's an in-breath and out-breath.

96
00:08:14,151 --> 00:08:19,512
[SPEAKER_01]: An action that is within my experience, and pretty much fundamental for any other action I do.

97
00:08:19,953 --> 00:08:21,753
[SPEAKER_01]: It precedes it, structurally, so to speak.

98
00:08:22,493 --> 00:08:24,634
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, I can stand up, walk, talk.

99
00:08:24,954 --> 00:08:27,095
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that requires breathing to be there.

100
00:08:29,635 --> 00:08:33,277
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you calm the saṅkhara, the determination of the body.

101
00:08:34,237 --> 00:08:36,998
[SPEAKER_01]: Through calming the determination of the body, which is in and out of breath,

102
00:08:39,554 --> 00:08:41,095
[SPEAKER_01]: All your bodily actions will come down.

103
00:08:41,815 --> 00:08:43,897
[SPEAKER_01]: All the pressures towards bodily actions will come down.

104
00:08:44,337 --> 00:09:02,008
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's a similar way to say that ānāpānasati, when practiced, made much of, you know, when you become proficient in it properly, through that, well, through that non-management, by the direction of calming of your pressure through it, it's like the rain that would sort of clean the air of all the dust particles.

105
00:09:05,755 --> 00:09:19,788
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't emphasize enough the point that, like, if you're doing it as a technique, as a method, as something you're doing, adhering, focusing hard in front of you, you're doing it at the expense of your container of endurance that you have developed.

106
00:09:20,188 --> 00:09:21,690
[SPEAKER_01]: You're doing it as an escape.

107
00:09:22,782 --> 00:09:26,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you're doing it because you're averse towards the discomfort of it.

108
00:09:27,543 --> 00:09:28,883
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you're doing it wrong.

109
00:09:29,503 --> 00:09:31,284
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's just the extension of sensuality.

110
00:09:31,704 --> 00:09:34,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what else is the principle of sensuality?

111
00:09:35,004 --> 00:09:43,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Is underlying tendency to aversion turns you towards getting rid of it by the means of anything that's not as averse or more pleasant.

112
00:09:43,946 --> 00:09:45,147
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the principle of sensuality.

113
00:09:45,187 --> 00:09:47,187
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where people go to sensual desires and so on.

114
00:09:48,347 --> 00:10:01,751
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha said that, when an ignoble mind is touched by an unpleasant feeling, doesn't know any other escape, doesn't know any other calming in regard to the discomfort it endures, other than turning towards sensuality.

115
00:10:01,831 --> 00:10:10,833
[SPEAKER_01]: But each time they turn to sensuality, more of the underlying tendency, because of which they were suffering on account of the original discomfort, will increase.

116
00:10:12,252 --> 00:10:15,475
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, the underlying tendency for depending on more pleasure will also increase.

117
00:10:16,055 --> 00:10:24,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the more you give in to the direction of trying to manage, get rid of your pain, get rid of the suffering through replacing it through any more pleasant experience, well, the worse it gets.

118
00:10:25,202 --> 00:10:26,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Like scratching the itch.

119
00:10:27,844 --> 00:10:29,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the whole point of sīla.

120
00:10:29,986 --> 00:10:34,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense restraint, virtue, moderation, watchfulness.

121
00:10:34,570 --> 00:10:35,751
[SPEAKER_01]: It's exactly that.

122
00:10:35,771 --> 00:10:39,674
[SPEAKER_01]: To provide you container that will not go to centrality.

123
00:10:40,977 --> 00:10:42,037
[SPEAKER_01]: They will reveal.

124
00:10:42,137 --> 00:10:48,100
[SPEAKER_01]: And literally, what you are averse to, it's no one's fault.

125
00:10:48,140 --> 00:10:51,581
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the setup of the aggregates, the setup of the senses.

126
00:10:52,382 --> 00:10:57,604
[SPEAKER_01]: They are basically... well, they're not pleasant in themselves.

127
00:10:58,064 --> 00:10:58,844
[SPEAKER_01]: Let's put it that way.

128
00:10:59,685 --> 00:11:01,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Having a body is a chore.

129
00:11:01,625 --> 00:11:07,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Having senses is... it's this resistance, natural resistance.

130
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,521
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are paired with, and you have to endure, whether you want to or not.

131
00:11:12,141 --> 00:11:16,282
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, like, see, you're not born with all this, like a newborn baby.

132
00:11:16,302 --> 00:11:23,924
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't have all this proliferous sensuality, but it has a setup of the senses, that in themselves are pressuring, unpleasant, resisting.

133
00:11:25,405 --> 00:11:32,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance, ignorant mind, and that means automatic direction towards pleasure, avoiding this.

134
00:11:34,244 --> 00:11:36,906
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that's the way we refer to the newborn baby stuff.

135
00:11:36,926 --> 00:11:38,467
[SPEAKER_01]: It has the same underlying tendencies.

136
00:11:38,507 --> 00:11:44,130
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it will become a mind dependent on sensuality, that doesn't want pain.

137
00:11:44,751 --> 00:11:58,740
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your patient endurance, your sīla, samādhi, is about to contain you trying to spill out towards senses, at the expense of the senses that are kind of unendurable, or so it feels.

138
00:11:59,280 --> 00:12:01,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And by the way, that's also why your will is paired with it.

139
00:12:02,456 --> 00:12:04,977
[SPEAKER_01]: You are already averse towards your own sense bases.

140
00:12:06,258 --> 00:12:08,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutrality is the aversion, because they are unpleasant.

141
00:12:10,320 --> 00:12:25,007
[SPEAKER_01]: If you don't endure that aversion, if you don't calm yourself in regard to the aversion of your own senses, you go out there for pleasure, which means you will go out there for any other kind of... that aversion will proliferate with you going out there with the pleasure.

142
00:12:25,707 --> 00:12:30,570
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people have ill will towards other people, because fundamentally they carry ill will towards their own senses.

143
00:12:31,613 --> 00:12:32,874
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the root of every evil.

144
00:12:34,416 --> 00:12:39,721
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of the aversion towards their own senses, they are dependent on sensuality through other people and the world.

145
00:12:40,462 --> 00:12:41,163
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same root.

146
00:12:42,584 --> 00:12:45,607
[SPEAKER_01]: So you uproot that root of aversion towards your own senses,

147
00:12:46,589 --> 00:12:52,473
[SPEAKER_01]: And you have lost any sensual desire or any will towards anywhere, anywhere in the world.

148
00:12:52,654 --> 00:12:54,595
[SPEAKER_00]: That doesn't make the senses comfortable.

149
00:12:54,735 --> 00:12:56,076
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no, no.

150
00:12:56,116 --> 00:13:08,665
[SPEAKER_01]: It just makes your mind go beyond this, that basic discomfort of the senses that you never ever addressed, because from the moment you were born, you, your mind was going in the direction of the world to avoid.

151
00:13:09,206 --> 00:13:10,707
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, that's why people fear boredom.

152
00:13:12,653 --> 00:13:16,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Boredom just brings back that which you've been outrunning from the day one.

153
00:13:18,314 --> 00:13:20,475
[SPEAKER_00]: The discomfort of your senses.

154
00:13:20,495 --> 00:13:21,735
[SPEAKER_00]: They are truly uncomfortable.

155
00:13:23,315 --> 00:13:31,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So, samatha is when you stop giving in blindly and holding views that are about management of that discomfort.

156
00:13:32,497 --> 00:13:37,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Samatha is calming of that discomfort after you have endured it sufficiently enough and developed clarity in regard to it.

157
00:13:39,068 --> 00:13:41,509
[SPEAKER_01]: So, calming the discomfort within the... How do you calm it?

158
00:13:41,549 --> 00:13:57,114
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, any satipaṭṭhāna would calm it, but it might be too abstract, like, you know, ah, my body's here, or something, too much activity of breath, that it's there all the time, from the day one as well, and it kind of precedes the senses.

159
00:13:57,494 --> 00:13:59,614
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't require your senses in order to breathe.

160
00:14:01,815 --> 00:14:04,276
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you cultivate it, make much of your breath

161
00:14:06,567 --> 00:14:10,649
[SPEAKER_01]: As calming of the endurance that it's not negotiable, that's arahantship.

162
00:14:11,469 --> 00:14:17,531
[SPEAKER_01]: In half a night, seven days, fortnight, seven months or seven years, at most.

163
00:14:18,912 --> 00:14:28,696
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the only reason you still depend on, you know, sensuality, distraction and so on, is because you're still unable to endure the core of the problem, which is the senses.

164
00:14:29,156 --> 00:14:34,118
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why an arahant, in the suttas, who becomes awakened, he says,

165
00:14:35,228 --> 00:14:35,968
[SPEAKER_01]: I've done all the work.

166
00:14:36,088 --> 00:14:39,810
[SPEAKER_01]: There's absolutely no more resistance, no more lust towards anything, anyone in the world, no more conceit, nothing left.

167
00:14:40,210 --> 00:14:44,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Any disturbance that's left for me is just on account of this six-sense body that's left until it breaks apart.

168
00:14:45,053 --> 00:14:46,553
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's no disturbance anymore.

169
00:14:48,234 --> 00:14:54,897
[SPEAKER_01]: See, because you're not actually... the senses are kind of uncomfortable, but they're not dukkha in themselves.

170
00:14:54,937 --> 00:14:59,960
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your resistance, aversion to discomfort on the deepest level of me.

171
00:15:01,173 --> 00:15:02,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the root of the problem.

172
00:15:02,553 --> 00:15:10,056
[SPEAKER_01]: This here is uncomfortable and automatically that discomfort means aversion for me.

173
00:15:11,696 --> 00:15:20,639
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, I must get rid of the senses, I must kill this body, I must... You're just gonna send yourself to who knows what other body.

174
00:15:20,719 --> 00:15:28,162
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a good opportunity to actually just train in regard to discomfort of the senses and not being averse towards it.

175
00:15:30,373 --> 00:15:32,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Through discovering calmness within it.

176
00:15:32,315 --> 00:15:32,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is.

177
00:15:32,995 --> 00:15:33,916
[SPEAKER_01]: Like eye of the storm.

178
00:15:35,497 --> 00:15:36,238
[SPEAKER_01]: But it takes effort.

179
00:15:36,458 --> 00:15:37,238
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to develop it.

180
00:15:37,759 --> 00:15:44,244
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you do, all of the fetters that depend on the world are gone.

181
00:15:44,284 --> 00:15:45,084
[SPEAKER_01]: Mind is unmoved.

182
00:15:45,104 --> 00:15:46,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what was moving the mind.

183
00:15:46,646 --> 00:15:48,787
[SPEAKER_01]: The aversion towards this thing that I don't want.

184
00:15:49,508 --> 00:15:49,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

185
00:15:50,108 --> 00:15:52,870
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else was secondary.

186
00:15:53,131 --> 00:15:56,793
[SPEAKER_01]: All the wrong views and all the sensuality, it was all secondary rooted in this.

187
00:16:02,759 --> 00:16:05,968
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the correct samatha.

